   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( h(x) = x^{2} \\). The functions f and g satisfy \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) = 2 \\) and \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} g(x) = -1 \\), and selected values of f and g are given in the table. Find \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{h(f(x))}{g(x)} \\).",
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -4
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Because h is a polynomial, it is continuous, so \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} h(f(x)) = h(2) = 4 \\).",
         "rule_named": "limit of a composite with a continuous outer function"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The limit of the denominator is -1, which is not 0, so the quotient theorem applies.",
         "rule_named": "quotient limit theorem, denominator condition"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "\\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{h(f(x))}{g(x)} = \\frac{4}{-1} = -4 \\). The tabulated values at x = 2 are not used, because f and g are not continuous there.",
         "mathjson": -4,
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99004",
         "rule_named": "quotient limit theorem"
      }
